WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.349 --> 00:00:02.970
- [Crowd] 12, 11, 10,

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:06.480
- So June 29th, 2007, we were excited.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:08.439
We were terrified.

00:00:08.439 --> 00:00:10.443
- [Woman] nine, eight, seven,

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:13.200
- It's a happy moment, but
it's also a stressful one.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:15.420
What's gonna happen when
it goes out into the world?

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.283
- [Crowd] six, five,

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.110
- You don't know what's about to come.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.760
This is a totally new experience

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:23.351
and something that's gonna
change our lives forever.

00:00:23.351 --> 00:00:24.840
- [Crowd] four, three,
(rousing orchestral music)

00:00:24.840 --> 00:00:25.673
- It was amazing.

00:00:25.673 --> 00:00:26.700
I mean, it still just kinda gives me

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.810
a little bit of goosebumps

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:29.070
because it was history.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:29.933
- [Crowd] two, one.

00:00:31.074 --> 00:00:32.470
(crowd cheers)

00:00:32.470 --> 00:00:34.710
- [Jason] A lot of nervousness,

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:36.510
a lot of stomach ache,

00:00:36.510 --> 00:00:38.281
a lot of excitement.
(Lauren laughs)

00:00:38.281 --> 00:00:39.930
(crowd cheers)

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.190
- I'm Lauren Schmick.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:42.480
- And I'm Jason Schmick.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.370
- And our son, Noah, was born

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:47.580
the same day the very
first iPhone was released.

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:50.040
- [Joanna] This is the
story of the iPhone baby

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:53.365
and how 15 years shaped a generation.

00:00:53.365 --> 00:00:56.490
(orchestral music)

00:00:56.490 --> 00:00:59.580
Try to remember life before the iPhone.

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:00.690
It's hard,

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.390
but Noah Schmick literally can't

00:01:03.390 --> 00:01:05.970
because, well, he's never
lived a day in the world

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:07.800
without an iPhone.

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.870
But this isn't just about one teenager

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:12.630
who shares his birthday
with a world-changing piece

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:14.100
of glass and metal.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.250
- I used to post this on
your birthday every year.

00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:19.980
It's because it's Steve Jobs
wishing you a happy birthday.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.970
Because look at it,

00:01:20.970 --> 00:01:23.135
you got the cake and
your birthday down there.

00:01:23.135 --> 00:01:24.208
- [Noah] The whole numbers right there.

00:01:24.208 --> 00:01:25.041
It's personalized.

00:01:25.041 --> 00:01:26.160
- [Jason] He cared.
(Lauren chuckles)

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:28.860
- [Joanna] This is about
how, over the last 15 years,

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.340
the iPhone itself went
from a baby to a teen.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:33.750
As it grew and developed,

00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:36.180
so did our dependence on it.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:39.300
Noah's generation is the
most extreme representation

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.770
of that dependence

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:43.440
because they've never
known life without it.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.590
It's a generation that
fears being disconnected.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:48.300
- This is everything you'd ever need

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:49.950
in the palm of your hands.

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:52.080
Why would you never be off of it?

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:54.780
They have the sum of the
world's entire knowledge

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.270
and all of your closest people

00:01:57.270 --> 00:01:58.950
instantly available to talk to.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:00.474
If you gave this thing to a caveman,

00:02:00.474 --> 00:02:02.146
they'd freak out.

00:02:02.146 --> 00:02:05.010
- [Joanna] Of course today,
it's not just the iPhone,

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:06.720
it's all smartphones.

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.450
- [Voiceover 1] Galaxy S22
Ultra breaks all the rules

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:11.130
of what a smartphone can do.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:12.240
- [Joanna] Legacy phone makers,

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:15.570
such as Samsung, Nokia,
and Blackberry creator RIM,

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:16.890
were forced to adapt

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:19.830
or die after the
introduction of the iPhone.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:21.210
- [Voiceover 2] So you thought Blackberrys

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:22.380
were all business.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:24.757
- [Joanna] while it was Google's
Android operating system

00:02:24.757 --> 00:02:25.800
- [Robotic voiceover] Droid.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.390
- [Joanna] combined with
more affordable phones

00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:28.620
from Samsung and others

00:02:28.620 --> 00:02:31.200
that captured the biggest
global market share,

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.480
it was Apple's iPhone
that really kicked off

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:34.743
the mobile revolution.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.260
So I set out to pinpoint
the major developments

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:42.690
to the iPhone that led to that dependence

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.510
with exclusive access to Apple executives,

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.903
past and present, who made
this revolutionary product.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.870
Alongside those conversations,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.070
I went to Wisconsin.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:54.600
Hi! Joanna.

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.430
And I traced the story of Noah,

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.500
his twin 13-year-old siblings,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.360
and his parents.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.520
Just like millions of other families,

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:05.220
Apple's invention made them more connected

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.048
and disconnected in
ways no one anticipated.

00:03:09.048 --> 00:03:10.080
(light music)

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:10.913
- Look!

00:03:10.913 --> 00:03:12.570
Baby's first picture.

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.760
And this blob is his head.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.650
- But look, he has his hand up by his face

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.210
and that's exactly how he was born,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.043
with his hand.

00:03:19.043 --> 00:03:20.520
- [Joanna] While Noah was in development,

00:03:20.520 --> 00:03:22.560
Apple was developing its own baby

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.200
over 21,000 miles away.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:27.600
- The start of the iPhone project happened

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.640
from the iPod project.

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:32.640
We started seeing these feature phones

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:36.210
with cameras starting to
add digital music features.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:37.770
- [Voiceover 3] A hundred
tunes in your phone, baby,

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:38.700
- [Voiceover 4] Hello, Moto.

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:41.820
- And it was clear at some point

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:44.880
they might be able to catch up with us

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.860
with this lead that we had

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.060
with the iPod.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:49.290
- [Voiceover 5] Imagine a phone that is

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:51.870
all music on one side
and all talk on the other

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:52.770
just like you.

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:56.040
- A mobile phone wanted
to have everything.

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:58.410
It wanted to be all-in-one device.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:00.097
At the time, we were also saying,

00:04:00.097 --> 00:04:02.760
"Okay, what is the future of the iPod?

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.370
And if people have two
devices in their hands,

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:07.650
which one would they pick up every time?"

00:04:07.650 --> 00:04:10.170
- [Joanna] After Tony
Fadell co-invented the iPod,

00:04:10.170 --> 00:04:12.450
Steve Jobs had a new task for him

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:14.790
and a few others in early 2005,

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:18.060
figure out what an Apple
phone would look like.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.610
- One was a iPod plus phone,

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:23.550
which looked like an iPod Classic.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.660
It had the wheel on it.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:27.330
We tried for weeks, six, eight weeks,

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:28.740
to try to crack the code on that.

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:30.480
We couldn't make it work.

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:34.140
There was another project we
were doing in the iPod team.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.990
We were making a full screen iPod.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:39.210
And the full screen iPod
was we were playing videos

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:40.410
at the time on the iPod,

00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:41.610
but it was in a smaller screen.

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:44.580
Then over in the world of the Mac,

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:47.010
they were making multi-touch technology.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:49.830
All three of those things
came together over time

00:04:49.830 --> 00:04:51.720
to create what we know as the iPhone.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.350
- Today, we're introducing

00:04:55.350 --> 00:04:59.370
three revolutionary
products of this class.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:03.150
- [Joanna] By January 2007, it
was ready to show the world.

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:05.910
- A wide screen iPod with touch controls,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.070
a revolutionary mobile phone,

00:05:08.070 --> 00:05:11.322
and a breakthrough internet
communications device.

00:05:11.322 --> 00:05:12.780
(audience cheering)

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:16.358
These are not three separate devices.

00:05:16.358 --> 00:05:18.880
(audience cheering loudly)
This is one device

00:05:23.059 --> 00:05:25.083
and we are calling it iPhone.

00:05:26.841 --> 00:05:28.110
(audience cheering)

00:05:28.110 --> 00:05:29.280
- My group that I worked with,

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:32.040
we were all big Mac fanatics.

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:34.320
Every one of us, our
jaws just hit the floor.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:36.847
We're like, this is the
coolest thing we've ever seen.

00:05:36.847 --> 00:05:38.850
I knew I had to have one. (chuckles)

00:05:38.850 --> 00:05:40.320
- [Joanna] But instead of standing in line

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.090
for that very first iPhone,

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.340
Jason Schmick grabbed his digital camera,

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:45.900
iPod, and Blackberry

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:47.490
and headed to the birthing center

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:50.280
right after he fired off
an email to his colleague.

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.757
- Well, I titled it Red Alert.

00:05:51.757 --> 00:05:53.520
"It looks like the iPhone
isn't the only thing

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:54.900
being released today.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:56.100
I will not be in today.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.870
Lauren went into labor last night."

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.610
- [Joanna] Around 6:00 PM in Madison,

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.280
the iPhone and Noah were
released to the world.

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:04.440
- [Jason] Here he is everyone.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.630
Noah Harrison Schmick.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.550
- Was there anything surprising to you

00:06:08.550 --> 00:06:09.990
as the phone gets out into the world

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:12.030
about how it starts to be used?

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.060
- The culture at Apple
changed dramatically.

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:16.890
Because the people who had them,

00:06:16.890 --> 00:06:20.670
they were always messaging
or they were always emailing.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:21.900
When people were in the meetings,

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:23.160
they were doing this all the time.

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:26.850
It became a constant deluge
of emails and messages.

00:06:26.850 --> 00:06:27.930
And you were like, "Oh my God,

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:29.250
no one could get off of them."

00:06:29.250 --> 00:06:31.770
And you were like,
something's different here.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:33.900
- [Joanna] But messaging,
phone calls, and music

00:06:33.900 --> 00:06:35.340
weren't the end game.

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:38.460
What came next really changed things.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.660
- As we arrive at iPhone's first birthday,

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.540
we're gonna take it to the next level.

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:49.528
And today we're introducing the iPhone 3G.

00:06:49.528 --> 00:06:50.720
(audience cheering)

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.920
- The year I got my first iPhone was 2008

00:06:55.309 --> 00:06:57.060
and it was the 3G.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:58.560
- So it looked something like this.

00:06:58.560 --> 00:06:59.943
- Oh my God, yes.

00:07:01.061 --> 00:07:05.970
(laughs) I forgot how
curved the bottom was.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.970
- [Joanna] The second iPhone
had a faster 3G connectivity.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.640
That speed combined with
a new software feature

00:07:11.640 --> 00:07:14.430
turn the iPhone into
something more than a phone.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:15.900
- [Voiceover 6] There's an app for that.

00:07:15.900 --> 00:07:20.160
In 2008, the iPhone 3G
brought faster 3G networking

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.080
and the revolutionary App Store.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:24.300
- [Joanna] Greg Joswiak
was part of a small team

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.410
Steve Jobs tasked with rolling
out the iPhone to the masses.

00:07:28.410 --> 00:07:30.360
He now leads marketing for Apple.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.000
- You know, the other great
innovation was in 2008.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:34.020
It was the App Store.

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:36.540
That is what created this massive

00:07:36.540 --> 00:07:38.760
set of things that is what
changed our lives, right?

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:40.020
They didn't all just come from us.

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.340
They came from what we did

00:07:41.340 --> 00:07:42.810
to empower developers.

00:07:42.810 --> 00:07:45.360
- When I joined Apple in March 2009,

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:48.010
there were roughly about 8,000,

00:07:48.010 --> 00:07:50.250
6,000 apps submitted per week.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:51.870
I mean, it grown quickly.

00:07:51.870 --> 00:07:53.580
- [Joanna] Oh yes, the store exploded

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:55.380
beyond Apple's expectations.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.950
And so the company hired
Phillip Shoemaker to oversee it.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:02.370
- 2008 Christmas, one developer
made a million dollars

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.590
and that issued

00:08:04.590 --> 00:08:06.180
a gold rush to everybody

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.070
and developers started submitting in mass.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.660
When the App Store launched,

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:12.090
Apple actually expected maybe 500 apps.

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:15.570
By April, we were at 25,000 apps per week.

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:17.010
- [Joanna] There were all kinds of apps.

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.230
Ones that would turn your
phone into a flashlight,

00:08:19.230 --> 00:08:20.370
a restaurant critic,

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:22.170
a personalized DJ,

00:08:22.170 --> 00:08:23.310
and loads that would, well,

00:08:23.310 --> 00:08:25.530
turn your phone into a babysitter.

00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.710
- We started to hand Noah the phone

00:08:28.710 --> 00:08:30.630
shortly after the twins came along.

00:08:30.630 --> 00:08:34.050
So he was two, maybe three.

00:08:34.050 --> 00:08:37.140
Be entertained for a
minute at a restaurant

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.770
or I am breastfeeding.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:43.620
He knew where the page of games was

00:08:43.620 --> 00:08:45.120
that he was allowed to play.

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:46.980
And even if it was a hit the chicken

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:48.426
and it makes a chicken sound

00:08:48.426 --> 00:08:49.260
(chicken clucks)

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:51.420
to him that was the greatest
thing in the whole world.

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.700
- I never thought that
kids apps would take off.

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:55.200
The iPhone was,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:56.276
for those of us that have been

00:08:56.276 --> 00:08:59.190
in the phone space for a while,

00:08:59.190 --> 00:09:01.110
seemingly kind of difficult to use, right?

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.300
People didn't understand how to swipe,

00:09:03.300 --> 00:09:05.970
how to input text, et cetera, et cetera.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:07.200
But I was wrong.

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:08.490
- [Joanna] The days of bringing toys

00:09:08.490 --> 00:09:11.280
and books and crayons to
the restaurant were over.

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:14.310
Now, games and apps were just a tap away.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:15.960
But that instant gratification

00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:20.490
had some unforeseen and
expensive consequences.

00:09:20.490 --> 00:09:23.760
- In 2009, late 2009, my
daughter's five years old.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:24.720
Her name is Miley.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:26.550
She started playing Smurfs a lot.

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.110
About a month later, we realized

00:09:28.110 --> 00:09:31.530
that she had purchased
$450 worth of credits,

00:09:31.530 --> 00:09:32.498
Smurf berry.

00:09:32.498 --> 00:09:33.930
(coins clanking)

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:36.300
So we instituted something
we called the Miley rule,

00:09:36.300 --> 00:09:39.360
which was a feature in
the operating system

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:42.270
that allowed the parent
to turn off purchases,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:44.790
saying requires a password every time.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.460
- [Joanna] By the time Noah's
generation was four or five,

00:09:47.460 --> 00:09:49.260
they were immersed in digital worlds

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.000
that almost felt real.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:52.200
As for the real world,

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:54.090
big leaps in the iPhone's camera

00:09:54.090 --> 00:09:57.090
were starting to give them
a whole new way to see it.

00:09:57.090 --> 00:10:00.060
- We set out to create an all new phone,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:02.490
a camera in the iPhone 4S.

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:04.320
We didn't set our sights
in just making it better

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.670
than every other phone.

00:10:05.670 --> 00:10:07.110
We've already done that.

00:10:07.110 --> 00:10:09.240
We set our sights on competing

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:12.042
with the quality of many
great point and shoot cameras.

00:10:12.042 --> 00:10:13.482
(upbeat music)

00:10:13.482 --> 00:10:15.450
(Lauren laughing)

00:10:15.450 --> 00:10:16.770
- This is one part where you like

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:18.933
start really shaking (chuckles) your butt.

00:10:21.270 --> 00:10:22.523
There it is.

00:10:22.523 --> 00:10:25.320
(Lauren laughing)

00:10:25.320 --> 00:10:27.960
- You literally dance like
that for the whole song

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:29.449
and still were not tired.

00:10:29.449 --> 00:10:30.282
(all laughing)

00:10:30.282 --> 00:10:31.470
I mean, it's your first kid.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.810
You take a photo of him doing anything

00:10:33.810 --> 00:10:37.260
and 'cause you think
everything they do is amazing.

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.810
I think the other thing that really

00:10:39.810 --> 00:10:41.850
kicked it into high gear
for us using the camera

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.310
was when Facebook became an app

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:48.300
because that was how we shared
everything with my family.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:50.390
And my parents wanted to see photos

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:52.421
of our kids all the time.

00:10:52.421 --> 00:10:55.530
(magical music)

00:10:55.530 --> 00:10:56.700
- [Joanna] With faster uploading

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:58.560
and improved social media apps,

00:10:58.560 --> 00:10:59.910
Noah decided he wanted to be

00:10:59.910 --> 00:11:02.269
in more than just family photos.

00:11:02.269 --> 00:11:03.870
(swoosh loudly)

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:07.140
- I started watching YouTube
when I was like six or seven.

00:11:07.140 --> 00:11:09.660
I wanted to be a YouTuber very badly.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.370
- And did you ever try to shoot videos

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.070
on your parents' iPhones or your phone?

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:16.170
- Yeah, I would drag my parents out

00:11:16.170 --> 00:11:21.170
to record these terrible,
terrible tutorial videos

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:23.760
for just toys I got for holidays.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.363
He's kind of big

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:30.030
even though on the back he's all stuffy.

00:11:30.030 --> 00:11:32.340
- [Joanna] But soon, kids
wouldn't even need their parents

00:11:32.340 --> 00:11:34.080
to hold the camera anymore.

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:36.510
- The next phone has a front-facing camera

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.970
and we are trying to figure out

00:11:38.970 --> 00:11:40.680
what do you do with the
front-facing camera?

00:11:40.680 --> 00:11:42.390
And you can take a picture
with the front-facing camera.

00:11:42.390 --> 00:11:43.920
You take a picture of yourself.

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:44.940
Is that gonna be big?

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:45.780
I don't know.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:47.220
- It was.
- It ends up being

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:48.840
- Spoiler.
big deal.

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:51.600
- [Joanna] Justin Santamaria
was a senior engineer at Apple

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:53.820
working on iOS and led the team

00:11:53.820 --> 00:11:56.520
that launched FaceTime in 2010.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.300
- I remember having the
vice president of iPhone

00:12:00.300 --> 00:12:02.317
come to my desk and say,

00:12:02.317 --> 00:12:03.990
"Steve wants to see a demo."

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:06.120
It's really funny as
Steve's kind of looking,

00:12:06.120 --> 00:12:08.670
he is holding his phone
like this looking down.

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:09.637
And I remember this, he goes,

00:12:09.637 --> 00:12:10.890
"I look abominable."

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:13.140
It's ultimately not how
people hold their phones today

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.330
for selfies or video calls.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:16.163
- [Joanna] That's right.

00:12:16.163 --> 00:12:18.060
Not even Steve Jobs saw the impact

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:20.370
of the front-facing camera coming.

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:23.760
Noah though, has grown up
surrounded by kids and adults

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.650
without stretched arms.

00:12:25.650 --> 00:12:29.130
- I do not like to take selfies of myself

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:32.220
because I don't understand
the appeal fully,

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.200
but my siblings definitely do.

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:36.210
They take photos of
themselves all the time.

00:12:36.210 --> 00:12:38.250
They'll do like an interlocked arm thing

00:12:38.250 --> 00:12:40.260
once they can fixture the other one.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:41.880
- I went on vacation

00:12:41.880 --> 00:12:44.160
and you saw people just taking selfies

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:45.720
all day long on vacation

00:12:45.720 --> 00:12:47.040
when you're at a resort or whatever,

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:48.697
and they're not enjoying
the world around it.

00:12:48.697 --> 00:12:49.950
And you're like, wait a second.

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:51.300
What's going on here?

00:12:51.300 --> 00:12:54.270
We are just intermediating reality

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:56.160
with this screen in front of your face.

00:12:56.160 --> 00:12:57.810
- I think it's just,

00:12:57.810 --> 00:13:01.380
society has trained them to
take pictures of themselves.

00:13:01.380 --> 00:13:03.780
- [Joanna] The iPhone was
no longer just a phone.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:07.020
It was a powerful, connected
computer with a camera.

00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:08.940
But one thing held this device back

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:12.090
from being something we
could do more and more on.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.740
- Today, we are launching

00:13:16.230 --> 00:13:21.230
the biggest advancement
in the history of iPhone.

00:13:21.237 --> 00:13:25.144
the iPhone 6 and the iPhone 6 Plus.

00:13:25.144 --> 00:13:26.700
(audience cheering)

00:13:26.700 --> 00:13:28.440
And yes, they're bigger.

00:13:28.440 --> 00:13:29.940
They're a lot bigger.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:33.990
- [Joanna] By 2014, the
smartphone competition was huge.

00:13:33.990 --> 00:13:34.823
Literally.

00:13:34.823 --> 00:13:35.656
- [Man 1] Check out the screen.

00:13:35.656 --> 00:13:36.540
This thing is huge.

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:37.373
- [Man 2] It's pretty massive.

00:13:37.373 --> 00:13:39.270
- [Joanna] Android
phone makers, especially

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:40.103
- Samsung.

00:13:40.103 --> 00:13:40.936
- Samsung?

00:13:40.936 --> 00:13:42.090
- That's says Samsung.

00:13:42.090 --> 00:13:44.190
- [Joanna] began packing
as much into these phones

00:13:44.190 --> 00:13:45.420
as possible.

00:13:45.420 --> 00:13:47.910
And with that, the phones
started getting bigger.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:50.220
- Would you mind moving
your enormous phone?

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.440
- You mean the enormously awesome Galaxy?

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.490
- Thinking back at that time,

00:13:53.490 --> 00:13:56.340
how big of a factor
was Samsung and Android

00:13:56.340 --> 00:13:59.310
as they were hitting the market
with these massive phones?

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:00.450
- They were annoying.

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:01.890
And they were annoying
because as you know,

00:14:01.890 --> 00:14:03.300
they ripped off our technology.

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:05.130
They took the innovations
that we had created

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.920
and created a poor copy of it

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:09.210
and just put a bigger screen around it.

00:14:09.210 --> 00:14:11.880
And so yeah, we were not too pleased.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.250
- [Joanna] Apple sued Samsung
for patent infringement.

00:14:14.250 --> 00:14:15.570
And by the end of the decade,

00:14:15.570 --> 00:14:17.550
Apple had received hundreds of millions

00:14:17.550 --> 00:14:19.980
for the patent violations.

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:22.830
A Samsung spokeswoman said
the company has pioneered

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.900
many mobile industry first,

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:25.920
- [Voiceover 7] Even in the rain,

00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.300
the shower or the pool.

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:29.250
- [Joanna] including large OLED displays

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:32.460
and water and dust resistant devices.

00:14:32.460 --> 00:14:35.130
By 2014, Apple finally caved

00:14:35.130 --> 00:14:37.860
and the iPhone was now
the size of a Samsung

00:14:37.860 --> 00:14:40.050
because Jason and millions of others

00:14:40.050 --> 00:14:42.540
saw the benefit of giant phones.

00:14:42.540 --> 00:14:44.198
- And yeah, I love this thing, man.

00:14:44.198 --> 00:14:46.721
And I said I was never gonna use a phone

00:14:46.721 --> 00:14:48.630
with a screen this big.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:51.570
- [Joanna] Two years later,
shortly after Noah turned 11,

00:14:51.570 --> 00:14:55.230
Jason handed that 6S Plus down to his son.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:57.267
- I was very excited when
I got my first iPhone.

00:14:57.267 --> 00:15:02.267
It was probably one of the
happiest moments in my life.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:05.880
I just remember getting it

00:15:05.880 --> 00:15:07.890
and then just running away immediately

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:09.180
to go play stuff.

00:15:09.180 --> 00:15:11.400
'Cause a lot of my
experience with phone games

00:15:11.400 --> 00:15:15.960
was at that point from
just watching my dad.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.090
- [Joanna] Yes, for Noah, it meant games.

00:15:18.090 --> 00:15:20.070
Well, even more games.

00:15:20.070 --> 00:15:23.250
He'd already played on his
computer and gaming console,

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:25.200
but now it was always with him,

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.450
his very own iPhone.

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:30.120
- Plants vs. Zombies
was a huge one for me.

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:31.530
Minecraft: Pocket Edition,

00:15:31.530 --> 00:15:34.261
like a lot of the original
phone games like Flappy Bird.

00:15:34.261 --> 00:15:35.094
(bird chimes)

00:15:35.094 --> 00:15:36.198
Angry Bird's a huge one.

00:15:36.198 --> 00:15:37.613
(glass breaks)

00:15:37.613 --> 00:15:38.446
(pig grunts)

00:15:38.446 --> 00:15:40.440
- I would say by the time Noah was 10,

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:45.440
he was pretty fully ingrained
in the video game world.

00:15:45.450 --> 00:15:48.030
It has never been my favorite thing

00:15:48.030 --> 00:15:49.920
and it is something that half of us

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:53.310
have tried to set limits on.
(both chuckles)

00:15:53.310 --> 00:15:54.600
- [Joanna] During the late 2010s,

00:15:54.600 --> 00:15:56.340
the iPhone not only grew in size

00:15:56.340 --> 00:15:58.110
but in processing power.

00:15:58.110 --> 00:16:01.470
More power equaled more capabilities.

00:16:01.470 --> 00:16:04.050
- On my phone now, which is this one,

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:07.980
I'm mostly just watch
YouTube and listen to music.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:09.840
I play games and browse Reddit and stuff,

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:14.100
but I don't really use it for phone stuff.

00:16:14.100 --> 00:16:16.830
It's just like a small
computer in my pocket.

00:16:16.830 --> 00:16:17.663
- [Joanna] After all that

00:16:17.663 --> 00:16:18.570
there wasn't much left to do

00:16:18.570 --> 00:16:21.330
to make the iPhone more
prevalent in our lives,

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:24.060
well, other than make
it do all those things

00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:25.830
for even longer.

00:16:25.830 --> 00:16:27.870
- We are so excited to tell you

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:29.610
that with iPhone 11 Pro

00:16:29.610 --> 00:16:33.176
you now get up to four
hours longer in your day.

00:16:33.176 --> 00:16:34.280
(audience cheering)

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:37.200
And with iPhone 11 Pro Max

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.340
up to five hours longer in your day.

00:16:39.340 --> 00:16:40.230
(audience applauding)

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.660
- Battery life's always
important for a portable product.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:46.290
We've made it even a bigger
and bigger point of emphasis.

00:16:46.290 --> 00:16:48.480
Whether it means making the
silicone more efficient,

00:16:48.480 --> 00:16:51.510
our ability to where we can
increase the size of battery

00:16:51.510 --> 00:16:52.620
and certainly larger screen,

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:54.393
iPhones allow you to do that.

00:16:56.015 --> 00:16:57.150
- Wow.

00:16:57.150 --> 00:16:58.620
- [Joanna] Ahead of his 15th birthday,

00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:01.980
Noah's parents finally
upgraded his iPhone 6S Plus

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.930
to a new iPhone 13.

00:17:03.930 --> 00:17:04.770
- This is cool, Dad.

00:17:04.770 --> 00:17:06.000
Thank you.

00:17:06.000 --> 00:17:09.210
Biggest difference is
definitely battery life.

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.900
Like on this phone, I could
probably only play a game

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:16.080
for two hours before
I'd have to plug it in.

00:17:16.080 --> 00:17:17.850
This one I can play a
game for an entire day

00:17:17.850 --> 00:17:20.070
and still get home after school day

00:17:20.070 --> 00:17:22.080
and be at 50% battery.

00:17:22.080 --> 00:17:23.010
It's amazing.

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.300
It's so much nicer.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:25.800
- Do you have a sense of how much time

00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:26.730
you spend on your phone?

00:17:26.730 --> 00:17:30.720
- I feel like not a healthy amount.

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:31.553
- [Joanna] That's Chloe,

00:17:31.553 --> 00:17:33.570
one of Noah's 13-year-old siblings

00:17:33.570 --> 00:17:36.210
who is especially
attached to her iPhone 12

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:38.550
and apps like TikTok and Snapchat.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:42.270
- I kind of feel like
I'm always on a device

00:17:42.270 --> 00:17:44.940
besides when I go
hanging out with friends.

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:48.030
And even then I'm on my phone sometimes.

00:17:48.030 --> 00:17:51.870
But I'm mostly on my phone and stuff.

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:52.703
- [Joanna] Like all of us,

00:17:52.703 --> 00:17:53.820
the Schmick's have enjoyed each

00:17:53.820 --> 00:17:55.560
of those life changing innovations

00:17:55.560 --> 00:17:58.650
that have come along with the
last 15 years of the iPhone.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:02.070
But now, possibly the greatest
innovation Apple can give us,

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:04.620
limiting how much we use it.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:06.720
- Screen Time empowers you

00:18:06.720 --> 00:18:09.000
with both insight and control

00:18:09.000 --> 00:18:10.467
over how you spend your time.

00:18:10.467 --> 00:18:14.490
And we think this is gonna
be helpful for many people,

00:18:14.490 --> 00:18:16.800
but especially for some kids.

00:18:16.800 --> 00:18:18.210
- We use Screen Time a lot

00:18:18.210 --> 00:18:21.720
to limit how much time they
get with certain things.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:25.200
So having it locked down
during the school day

00:18:25.200 --> 00:18:26.820
so they're not using it at school,

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:28.200
which they're not supposed to do anyways.

00:18:28.200 --> 00:18:29.253
- [Lauren] And at night to force them

00:18:29.253 --> 00:18:30.540
- [Jason] Yes.
to actually go to sleep.

00:18:30.540 --> 00:18:33.600
- Yes, they just simply don't work

00:18:33.600 --> 00:18:36.780
between the hours of nine to 6:00 AM

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:37.650
or something like that.

00:18:37.650 --> 00:18:38.483
- We try.

00:18:38.483 --> 00:18:40.620
You know, we're always, Chloe, especially,

00:18:40.620 --> 00:18:43.124
we definitely get a lot of time requests

00:18:43.124 --> 00:18:44.577
throughout the day.

00:18:44.577 --> 00:18:47.003
♪ Okay, all right ♪

00:18:47.003 --> 00:18:48.240
♪ It's about damn time ♪

00:18:48.240 --> 00:18:50.070
- [Lauren] And usually
followed by a text message

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:52.410
that says, "My teacher
said I can have 15 minutes.

00:18:52.410 --> 00:18:53.880
Can I please have 15 minutes?"

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:54.713
- Here we go.

00:18:54.713 --> 00:18:56.013
That's pretty.

00:18:56.013 --> 00:18:58.950
- I've been talking to a child

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.380
who was born on June 29th, 2007.

00:19:01.380 --> 00:19:02.670
This is him.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:03.503
- Oh!

00:19:03.503 --> 00:19:05.370
- That's his picture from June 29th, 2007.

00:19:05.370 --> 00:19:06.510
- Taken with the iPhone?

00:19:06.510 --> 00:19:08.370
- Not taken with the
iPhone as you'll learn,

00:19:08.370 --> 00:19:09.203
but,
- Bummer.

00:19:09.203 --> 00:19:10.260
- this is something he said to me

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:11.580
during our interview a couple weeks ago.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:13.680
He says, "This is everything
you would ever need

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.240
in the palm of your hands.

00:19:15.240 --> 00:19:17.220
Why would you ever be off of it?"

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:18.660
Is that what you hope to hear

00:19:18.660 --> 00:19:21.300
or does some part of that
make you feel uncomfortable?

00:19:21.300 --> 00:19:22.133
- It's mixed, right?

00:19:22.133 --> 00:19:25.050
We've created an incredible tool

00:19:25.050 --> 00:19:27.360
to help people with how they learn

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:28.740
and to help people how they work.

00:19:28.740 --> 00:19:30.630
To help people with how they communicate,

00:19:30.630 --> 00:19:32.160
how they're entertained.

00:19:32.160 --> 00:19:33.120
It's a powerful tool.

00:19:33.120 --> 00:19:34.620
But at the same time

00:19:34.620 --> 00:19:35.940
we wanna help people with the fact

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:37.860
that there's moderation needed.

00:19:37.860 --> 00:19:40.800
But we don't make our
money off engagement.

00:19:40.800 --> 00:19:43.140
There's no like, more you use your iPhone

00:19:43.140 --> 00:19:43.973
the more money we make.

00:19:43.973 --> 00:19:45.150
That isn't the way it works, right?

00:19:45.150 --> 00:19:46.950
We just want you to have
this great experience

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:49.140
and sometimes that does mean

00:19:49.140 --> 00:19:50.940
you gotta temper how
much you're gonna use it.

00:19:50.940 --> 00:19:52.590
You gotta moderate how
much you wanna use this.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:54.710
- Have you seen this with
some of your own kids?

00:19:54.710 --> 00:19:56.670
- We all have concerns around social media

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:57.990
and the effect on kids.

00:19:57.990 --> 00:20:00.018
My younger daughter, yeah, no,

00:20:00.018 --> 00:20:01.500
she and I have talked about it

00:20:01.500 --> 00:20:03.480
and she goes through periods

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:05.460
where she's using it too much

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:07.899
and then scales herself way back.

00:20:07.899 --> 00:20:10.399
(light music)

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:16.018
- Truly, we just thought this was gonna be

00:20:16.018 --> 00:20:19.380
a fun, easy to use thing when
you wanna do a few messages.

00:20:19.380 --> 00:20:22.183
Didn't think it was gonna
become the center of your life.

00:20:22.183 --> 00:20:24.930
- [Lauren] We have burgers,
we have fries kind of.

00:20:24.930 --> 00:20:27.288
We have, I don't think there's asparagus.

00:20:27.288 --> 00:20:28.560
- [Noah] There is, there is.

00:20:28.560 --> 00:20:30.120
- There we go, all right.

00:20:30.120 --> 00:20:32.850
- [Joanna] After 15 years
of living with the iPhone,

00:20:32.850 --> 00:20:35.760
we now have to figure out
how to live with it less.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:39.570
- In theory, we have a no
phones at the table for dinner.

00:20:39.570 --> 00:20:41.700
- [Joanna] If we've learned
anything from all this

00:20:41.700 --> 00:20:43.590
it's that our individual willpower

00:20:43.590 --> 00:20:47.790
is only challenged by increasing
technological advancements.

00:20:47.790 --> 00:20:50.100
- It's always amazing
that every time you think

00:20:50.100 --> 00:20:51.270
it's as good as it can get,

00:20:51.270 --> 00:20:54.180
we always try, manage a
way to make it better.

00:20:54.180 --> 00:20:56.700
- But if and when that better thing comes,

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:58.440
will we control it

00:20:58.440 --> 00:21:00.300
or will it control us?

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:01.421
- Thank you for cooking.

00:21:01.421 --> 00:21:03.930
- [Chloe] Thanks for cooking.

00:21:03.930 --> 00:21:04.763
- As I've grown older,

00:21:04.763 --> 00:21:07.800
I've gotten way, way, way
more into these things

00:21:07.800 --> 00:21:10.290
as so as the rest of the world.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:11.400
They've gotten a lot better

00:21:11.400 --> 00:21:14.250
and they're gonna keep
getting insanely good.

00:21:14.250 --> 00:21:17.010
Taking away screens is like
taking away my friends.

00:21:17.010 --> 00:21:19.710
No one can stop you from being
on your phone at all times

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:21.886
besides your own self-discipline.

00:21:21.886 --> 00:21:24.386
(light music)

